 good availability guidance for  any production system  lastly as i mentioned to protect against  a region outage consider using our cross  region  replication or cross region replicas  again you'll have a near real time copy  asynchronous replication which means  you'll have data available to you in  another region  should your primary region have an  outage and you can make that data  accessible  very quickly to applications to get back  online  looking now at how to get automatic  transparency and trusted security  controls  will start with access transparency  access transparency gives you near  real-time logs  when a google cloud administrator  accesses your content  cloud audit logs already provide  visibility into the actions of your own  administrators  so for example your administrators might  be creating a cloud sql instance  taking a few backups via our api all  this is logged today  however this audit trail typically or  previously stopped  once your cloud provider support or  engineering team is engaged we  previously didn't log those transactions  now when you open a support ticket with  our support team  with via access transparency you'll see  their actions  in your audit log as well for our manual  and targeted accesses again either by  support or engineering to get your  database back online  you can take this further with access  approval so beyond just logging  and viewing the access you can use the  access approval  feature to increase your level of  control which  by requiring explicit approvals before  google support or engineering can access  your data or configurations on google  cloud  i recommend taking a look at both access  transparency and access approval to see  how they fit with your environment  next up let's look at encryption and  specifically customer managed encryption  keys  these keys are intended for  organizations that have sensitive  or regulated data that requires them to  manage their own encryption key  these keys are stored in a key  management system  that you manage we're using keys in  cloud sql that you manage  and we access them as part of our  operations  should you remove access from that team  then when cloud sql performs a major  operation next  it won't be able to get the key and  access to that system is shut down  again this feature allows you to use  cryptographic keys that you manage  to control your data at rest the data we  store in cloud sql  i mentioned earlier google cloud's  global vpcs  we've introduced in cloud sql support  for vpc  service controls these generally in gcp  allow you to define very fine grade  perimeter controls  across numerous google cloud services of  course including cloud sql  for cloud sql this pairs very well with  vpcs or private ip addresses  with the combination of private ip  addresses and vpc service controls  you're now able to make private your  cloud sql instances both from a data  path perspective  as well as a control path perspective  the private ip or vpc  allows you to protect the data path the  things that your clients and  applications are connected to  by using a private ip address on a vpc  that you control  vpc service controls for cloud sql  protect the administrative api  who can create cloud sql instances who  can take backups via the api or the ui  for example  you're now able to make our  administration api totally private as  well  ensuring that you can control where your  your teams can access cloud sql  instances not just on the data path  but also on the control path of the  admin api  let's round out with how to stay up to  date without the risk  as a reminder cloud sql has long offered  maintenance windows  which are one hour per week times when  used you define that google can perform  maintenance on your instance  in general cloud sql typically performs  maintenance once a quarter on your  instances to apply patches and keep  things up to date  we would adhere to your maintenance  window as you see in this diagram  one thing to note about maintenance  windows is that we offer  certain timings if in a given project  you have dev and test  database instances as well as some  production database instances  you might put the dev and test databases  on the earlier timing  and you might put the production  databases on the later timing  this ensures then that the dev test  instances on the earlier timing  get maintenance or receive maintenance  about a week in advance of your  production instances  that way it gives you a chance to test  the updates with your application before  they go into your production environment  we've recently added maintenance  notification and rescheduling  when you opt in we'll we'll email you a  week in advance of maintenance  letting you know that we're planning to  perform maintenance in your next  maintenance window  you get a few options as part of this  notification including the ability to  reschedule to a following  window one week later or the ability to  apply maintenance anytime you click the  apply now button  you can also set a specific time anytime  in the next two weeks  to apply maintenance if you're not happy  with your current maintenance windows  schedule  this gives you much more control as to  when maintenance is applied  especially on your production instances  and lets your teams know well in advance  that maintenance will be performed to  wrap  up hopefully these features help you  find a bit more flexibility in your  environment with things like  bigquery federation and committed use  discounts  helping you protect against the  unexpected be that user error with point  in time recovery or  a regional outage with cross-region  replication and disaster recovery  they'll allow you to get automatic  transparency and trusted security  controls  things like private ip and now the  addition of epc security controls for  example  as well as customer managed encryption  keys and we'll round it out with our new  maintenance notifications and  rescheduling functionality  as you can see cloud sql is continuing  to build this momentum with respect to  enterprise features  and you can expect to see more of this  in future offerings  for next steps i encourage you to take a  look at two other sessions  the first a data monetization story  where mckesson talks about their journey  to google cloud  and please take a look at the bare metal  solution which  will help you bring your enterprise  workloads to gcp  if you want to see cloud sql in action  take a look at our showcase demo which  is a whack-a-mole style  high availability demonstration i  mentioned high availability briefly  in this demonstration as i mentioned  whack-a-mole style  you'll get to see how many cloud  sequences can you make fail  and how many recover in the amount of  time you're given in the game take a  look  lastly get hands-on with cloud sql and  try it out with our quick labs quest  in the quest you'll be introduced to  cloud sql from beginner knowledge all  the way through advanced  touching on a lot of the features that  i've talked about today by the end i  think you'll feel very comfortable using  cloud sql  and you'll hopefully be taking advantage  of some of the enhancements that we've  brought out this year  thank you all for spending this time  with me and watching the session as i  mentioned i'll be available as part of  our google cloud next expert sessions if  you want to meet one-on-one and talk a  little more about cloud sql  thank you again
 

